IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`

§ `Australia — Taylor v Johnson, as the Singapore Court of Appeal read it`

-- READ THIS BEFORE READING THE RULES. This module does NOT purport to state
-- Australian law. It encodes ONE decision -- Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR
-- 422 -- and it encodes it AS CHARACTERISED BY the Singapore Court of Appeal
-- in Chwee Kin Keong and others v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2 at
-- [72] and [73], because that is the source this corpus actually fetched and
-- can quote. The Australian reports were not consulted. Anything an
-- Australian lawyer would add -- later authority, the standing of the common
-- law doctrine in Australia, the dissent -- is missing by construction, and
-- the two open questions this creates are forks F-AU1 and F-AU2 in the fork
-- register.
--
-- It is here because Australia is the closest cousin Singapore has: the same
-- equitable instinct, reached through a differently-drawn test, and Chwee Kin
-- Keong departs from it in TWO places while relying on it in a third. Those
-- departures are the most interesting rows in the comparison table, and they
-- would be invisible without this module.

§§ `The facts the doctrine was drawn from`

-- Chwee Kin Keong at [72]: "the vendor by a written contract agreed to sell
-- two adjoining pieces of land, each of about five acres, for a total price
-- of $15,000. The vendor subsequently refused to proceed with the sale on the
-- ground that at the time she executed the contract she believed it provided
-- for a price of $15,000 per acre. ... the majority of the Australian High
-- Court affirmed the decision of the Court of Appeal on the ground that when
-- the purchaser executed the contract he believed the vendor was under some
-- serious mistake or misapprehension about either the price or the value of
-- the land and the purchaser had deliberately set out to ensure that the
-- vendor was not disabused of such a mistake or misapprehension."

§§ `The knowledge condition — no higher than Singapore's`

-- The purchaser in Taylor v Johnson did not know precisely what the vendor
-- had got wrong. Chwee Kin Keong at [73] records the High Court's answer:
-- "this was in their view a case of wilful ignorance because, knowing or
-- having reason to know that there was some mistake or misapprehension, the
-- purchaser engaged 'deliberately in a course of conduct which is designed to
-- inhibit discovery of it' (at 433)."
--
-- "Knowing or having reason to know" is the same rung Singapore's equitable
-- limb starts from. The divergence is not here.

@ref Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR 422 at 433, as quoted in Chwee Kin Keong at [73]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the purchaser knew or had reason to know of some mistake` IF
    `the other party at least had reason to suspect` c

§§ `The conduct condition — higher than Singapore's, and this is the divergence`

-- Taylor v Johnson (at 432), quoted in Chwee Kin Keong at [73], asks whether
-- the non-mistaken party "deliberately [set] out to ensure that the
-- [mistaken] party does not become aware of his mistake or misapprehension".
--
-- The Singapore Court of Appeal questioned exactly this, and then went
-- further than it (at [73]):
--
--   "What is less clear is whether it was an essential element for equity to
--   intervene that the non-mistaken party must 'deliberately [set] out to
--   ensure that the [mistaken] party does not become aware of his mistake or
--   misapprehension' (at 432). It seems to us that a conscious omission to
--   disabuse a mistaken party is itself sufficient to constitute
--   unconscionable conduct and thus justify the grant of equitable relief".
--
-- So on the test as stated in Taylor v Johnson, a party who merely says
-- nothing does not engage it; on Singapore's, he does. That single arm is the
-- first of the two Australian divergences, and the scenario battery isolates
-- it (see `Digilandmall -- the second appellant` in mistake-cases.l4).

@ref Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR 422 at 432, as quoted and questioned in Chwee Kin Keong at [73]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the purchaser deliberately set out to prevent discovery` IF
    CONSIDER c's `what the other party did`
    WHEN `deliberate steps to prevent discovery` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `The verdict — voidable, and never void`

-- The second divergence. Chwee Kin Keong at [73]: "To the extent that the
-- majority judgment seems to suggest that the contract would not be void even
-- where the non-mistaken party knew of a fundamental mistake, we would
-- hesitate to adopt a similar position."
--
-- On that reading, Australia routes even an actually-known fundamental
-- mistake to equity, where the remedy is rescission and the contract is
-- voidable, rather than to the common law, where it would be void. That
-- matters to a third party who buys from the non-mistaken party before
-- rescission: under a voidable contract he may keep what he bought, under a
-- void one he never had title at all. See `third-party consequences` in
-- mistake-comparison.l4, which is where that difference is made to bite.
--
-- "Seems to suggest" is the Court of Appeal hedging, and this model does not
-- un-hedge it. The reading encoded here is the one the source states; the
-- alternative -- that a common law void doctrine survives in Australia
-- alongside Taylor v Johnson -- is fork F-AU1, recorded as live and NOT
-- taken.

@export Decide what Taylor v Johnson (1983) 151 CLR 422 does with a contract one party entered into by mistake, as that decision was characterised by the Singapore Court of Appeal in Chwee Kin Keong. Not a general statement of Australian law.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`   @desc The facts of the case, as found: what the mistake was about, whether it was fundamental, what the other party knew, and what he did about it.
GIVETH A `What becomes of the contract`
DECIDE `what Taylor v Johnson does` c IS
    IF      (CONSIDER c's `what the mistaken party was mistaken about`
             WHEN `a term of the contract` THEN TRUE
             OTHERWISE FALSE)
        AND c's `the mistake was fundamental`
        AND `the purchaser knew or had reason to know of some mistake` c
        AND `the purchaser deliberately set out to prevent discovery` c
    THEN `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
    ELSE `the contract stands`
